RESULT
  Two hosts join DIFFERENT groups; both groups are sourced.  Each host receives ONLY
  the traffic of the group it joined - no client receives the other group's traffic.
      clientA (vlan82, joined G1) : G1=19/20   G2=0     [expect G1 only]
      clientB (vlan83, joined G2) : G1=0       G2=19/20 [expect G2 only]
      mroute (S,G1) outgoing list : vlan82 ONLY
      mroute (S,G2) outgoing list : vlan83 ONLY                          => PASS.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BENCH / TOPOLOGY AS TESTED   (same PIM-SM domain as T11405, u4 = RP)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DUT / PIM-SM router : u4  S/N 264A23052   RP = loopback 10.10.99.1
  Host (one NIC, tagged sub-ifs on u4 port1.0.2 trunk):
      source   : eth2.81  10.10.81.20  (vlan81)  - sources BOTH groups
      clientA  : eth2.82  10.10.82.20  (vlan82)  - joins G1 = 239.20.20.21
      clientB  : eth2.83  10.10.83.20  (vlan83)  - joins G2 = 239.20.20.22

METHOD / SCOPE
  No IXIA.  Each client joins a different group (scapy IGMPv2, on-subnet src).  The
  source transmits to BOTH groups.  BOTH clients are sniffed for BOTH groups, so a
  cross-leak would show as a non-zero count of the other client's group.  The
  per-(S,G) outgoing-interface list gives the same result from the control plane.
  Functional test.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG APPLIED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (identical to T11405: ip multicast-routing; service pim; ip pim rp-address
   10.10.99.1; vlan81/82/83 + lo each `ip pim sparse-mode`, vlan81/82/83 `ip igmp`.)

